---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout, DocCards, DocsTable } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const TEAM_ROWS = [
  ['<strong>NEXUS</strong>', 'Orchestrator', 'Picks up issues, coordinates the team, recovers from failures, and notifies you when input is needed.'],
  ['<strong>FORGE</strong>', 'Builder', 'Writes code against an agreed plan, creates branches, and opens pull requests.'],
  ['<strong>SENTINEL</strong>', 'Reviewer', 'Adversarially reviews the plan and the code for security, quality, and test coverage.'],
  ['<strong>VESSEL</strong>', 'DevOps', 'Watches CI, resolves merge conflicts, and merges green pull requests.'],
  ['<strong>LORE</strong>', 'Writer', 'Documents decisions and keeps project history up to date. Often disabled by default.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Introduction', href: '/docs/introduction' }, { label: 'What is OpenFlows' }]}>
  <h1>What is OpenFlows?</h1>

  <p>
    <strong>OpenFlows is an autonomous AI software team that turns GitHub issues into reviewed,
    production-ready pull requests — running entirely inside your own self-hosted Coder
    environment.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead of a single chatbot that suggests snippets, OpenFlows stands up a small team of
    specialized AI agents — each with a clear role — that plan, write, test, and adversarially
    review code on your behalf. You stay in control of architecture and final decisions; the
    team handles the execution.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="The one-line idea">
    You open a GitHub issue. OpenFlows plans it, writes it, stresses it, and merges it — then
    tells you what happened.
  </Callout>

  <h2>The founding principle</h2>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is built around a simple principle:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Coder decides <em>where</em> work runs. OpenFlows decides <em>how</em> agents work
    together.</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coder</strong> provides the governed, isolated, disposable workspaces and the model gateway where every AI call happens.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenFlows</strong> provides the coordination — the plan, the review, the merge, and the guardrails.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Neither replaces the other. OpenFlows does not try to manage machines, and Coder does not
    try to write your code.
  </p>

  <h2>The problem OpenFlows actually solves</h2>
  <p>
    Writing code in teams has become a bottleneck — not because teams got slower, but because a
    language model can now produce working code faster and more consistently than a person typing
    it by hand. Yet <strong>humans remain the product owners</strong>. They define what should be
    built and why, decide the architecture, and own the quality of what ships. The bottleneck the
    industry is now hitting is not coding speed — it is that <strong>people are sitting at the
    wrong phase of the pipeline</strong>, doing work that a machine could referee, while the work
    only a person can do goes undone.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the old review chain breaks down</h2>
  <p>
    Once agents are involved, the old chain — an agent writes, a human reviews — stops scaling. An
    agent can push far more code than any person can review by hand, but that code still needs
    review. The only first-pass reviewer that can keep up with an agent is <strong>another
    agent</strong>. In OpenFlows, review is an adversarial, isolated agent role, so humans are no
    longer the bottleneck that gates every pull request.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why a skilled engineer should not sit mid-pipeline clicking accept and deny on trivial
    changes day after day — work companies pay dearly for and that adds little judgment. That
    engineer is far more valuable <strong>one level up the chain</strong>: at product design and
    architecture, where human judgment cannot be replaced. Moving people to that level is not a
    cost cut; it is where they deliver the most value.
  </p>

  <h2>What problem does it solve?</h2>
  <p>
    Writing code against a specification has become cheap. What stays hard is <em>thinking</em>:
    understanding the architecture, making product judgments, and keeping security in mind.
    OpenFlows encodes that discipline as an automated team, so the engineering judgment gets
    applied to every issue, consistently — while removing the repetitive, low-judgment work and
    running it in a well-defined pipeline from start to end.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is it for?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Developers</strong> who want the mechanics of planning, coding, testing, and review handled while they stay in charge of the important decisions.</li>
    <li><strong>Companies</strong> that want governed, auditable AI delivery inside their existing engineering workflow — without exposing model keys or weakening security.</li>
    <li><strong>Stakeholders</strong> who want a faster, more transparent path from "we want this feature" to "it shipped."</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A quick tour of the team</h2>
  <DocsTable headers={['Agent', 'Role', 'What it does']} rows={TEAM_ROWS} />

  <p>
    The rest of this documentation explains how that team works, what it needs, and how to make
    it yours.
  </p>

